statements.statement:
For the reference-localized pilot selector in \(\text{def:spectral-band}\),
\[
 \sup_{(P,\bar g_n)\in\mathcal Q_{\mathrm{spec},n}}
 P\left\{\widehat j_n=\varnothing\text{ or }
 \inf_{v\in\mathcal I,z\in C_{\widehat j_n}}|F_{v,h_n}(z)|<\rho_F
 \text{ or }\widehat m_{\widehat j_n}\ne m_{\widehat j_n}
 \text{ or }m_{\widehat j_n}=0\right\}\longrightarrow0,
\]
where \(m_j=(2\pi i)^{-1}\oint_{C_j}M'(z)/M(z)\,dz\). The winding equality is derived from \(F_{v_{\mathrm{ref}},h_n}=MA_{v_{\mathrm{ref}},h_n}\) and the zero-free localized code factor; no global contaminated-transform claim is made.

statements.proof_tex:
\textbf{Proof.}
Choose \((p_k,a_k,\sigma_k)\in\mathfrak S_k\).  Since \(p_k\in(1/4,3/4)\),
\[
 \eta=a_k(B-p_k)\in\{-a_kp_k,a_k(1-p_k)\}
\]
is centered, bounded, independent of \((X,V)\), and nonzero almost surely.  Compatibility gives \(\|\eta\|_{\psi_2}\le\psi_\eta\), and direct differentiation of its log moment-generating function gives
\[
 \kappa_k(\eta)=a_k^k\left.\partial_t^k\log(1-p_k+p_ke^t)\right|_{t=0},
 \qquad |\kappa_k(\eta)|\ge\delta. \tag{1}
\]
This works for odd and even \(k\), because the selected asymmetric Bernoulli parameter avoids the finite zero set of the displayed cumulant polynomial.

Here \(V\) is uniform, so its density is one and compatibility states \(c_V\le1\le C_V\).  The choices \(g_0=q_0=\bar g_n=0\) obey the range restrictions.  Since \(0\in\mathcal G\), both the law-level approximation error and the supplied-code error equal zero, so the candidate and pair-membership requirements hold for every allowed positive radius.  The assumed H\"older and range restrictions on \(\tau_0\) supply the remaining curve conditions.

Conditional on \((X,V,T)\), \(T=\eta\) is fixed and \(\epsilon\) remains centered Gaussian.  Thus
\[
 E(\xi\mid X,V,T)=0,
 \quad
 \|\xi\|_{\psi_2\mid X,V,T}
 =|\eta|\,\|\epsilon\|_{\psi_2}
 \le a_k\sigma_k\max\{p_k,1-p_k\}\|N(0,1)\|_{\psi_2}
 \le\psi_\xi, \tag{2}
\]
and
\[
 E(\xi^2\mid X,V,T)=\eta^2\sigma_k^2
 \ge a_k^2\sigma_k^2\min\{p_k,1-p_k\}^2
 \ge\underline\sigma_\xi^2. \tag{3}
\]
All support, tail, separation, and nondegeneracy conditions of the primitive law class have now been checked.  Finally, \(T=\eta\ne0\) almost surely and
\[
 \frac{Y}{T}=\frac{\eta\{\tau_0(V)+\epsilon\}}{\eta}
 =\tau_0(V)+\epsilon. \tag{4}
\]
The joint law therefore factors into the ordinary uniform-design Gaussian regression experiment in (4) and the ancillary treatment variable \(T\), proving the proposition. \(\square\)

estimand_functional:
On reference-localized selector success and inference-denominator success,
\[
 \widehat\tau_n(v)=\operatorname{Re}\left\{
 (2\pi i\widehat m_{\widehat j_n})^{-1}
 \oint_{C_{\widehat j_n}}
 \frac{\widehat G_{2\widehat j_n,v}(z)}
 {\widehat F_{2\widehat j_n,v}(z)}\,dz\right\},
\]
where \(\widehat j_n\) and \(\widehat m_{\widehat j_n}\) are selected from the pilot reference-localized transform at \(v_{\mathrm{ref}}\). The half-width is \(\widehat c_{1-\alpha}\widehat s_n(v)/\sqrt{n_2h_n}+B_{\mathrm{bias}}L_\tau h_n^\beta\), with \(h_n\asymp(\log n/n)^{1/(2\beta+1)}\); on either selector or inference-denominator failure the total procedure returns \([-C_\tau,C_\tau]\). Its expected sup-width is \(\asymp(\log n/n)^{\beta/(2\beta+1)}\).
